# Working in __TITLE__

A light terminal app: one model over a JSONL file, one controller with live rows, and `View`s on
light's terminal host (`@lightstart/light/native/tui`), which `scriptc` compiles into one native
binary. Light's packages are linked from a checkout, so their READMEs are the reference.

## Scripts

| Command | Does |
|---|---|
| `pnpm dev` | `node src/main.ts`: the app on the terminal, `.data/todos.jsonl` by default |
| `pnpm test` | `node --test test/*.test.ts`: the app on light's test terminal, files in memory, no browser |
| `pnpm bake` | `light classes:bake`: rewrites `src/classes.baked.ts`, the classes the binary carries |
| `pnpm build` | `scriptc build src/main.ts -o dist/__NAME__ --strip`: one native binary |
| `pnpm check` | `light check`: light's conventions over the sources |

`dev` needs an interactive terminal. `bake` and `check` need TypeScript, a devDependency. The test
re-runs `classes:bake --check`, so a class a view gained without a fresh bake fails it.

## Where things go

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `src/main.ts` | The entry: `openTodos`, a `Terminal`, `runApp` |
| `src/models/` | One module per model: `defineModel(name, schema)` over `T`, and the row interface |
| `src/controllers/` | The app's state: a `Controller` subclass, its signals and its `liveRows` |
| `src/views/` | One module per view; a view a template names by tag calls `define(tag, Class)` |
| `src/lib/file-io.ts` | The JSONL adapter's files through `node:fs` |
| `src/classes.baked.ts` | Generated by `pnpm bake`; never edit by hand |
| `test/` | Node tests on light's test terminal |
| `tsconfig.json` | The editor's settings; `types: ["node"]` is what scriptc reads node's APIs from |

## A model

`defineModel` reads `T` descriptors. In TypeScript the schema is held in a `const schema:
ModelSchema` (the descriptor literal's own type does not compile natively), so the rows read as
dynamic data through `unknown` fields:

```ts
import { defineModel } from "@lightstart/light/model";
import T from "@lightstart/light/types";
import type { ModelSchema } from "@lightstart/light/model/types";

const schema: ModelSchema = {
  title: T.string({ required: true }),
  done: T.boolean({ defaultValue: false }),
};

export const todos = defineModel("todos", schema);
export interface Todo { id: unknown; title: unknown; done: unknown }
```

Read a field with `String(row.title)`; a field declared `required` or given a default is on every
stored row, and `id` is a string.

## A controller

In TypeScript a controller is a `Controller` subclass from `@lightstart/light/controller/class`
(`defineController`'s object literal does not lower from TypeScript). A signal field is a prop;
`liveRows(this, model, build)` is the live query, rebuilt whenever a signal `build` reads changes:

```ts
import { Controller } from "@lightstart/light/controller/class";
import { liveRows } from "@lightstart/light/model/binder";
import { signal } from "@lightstart/light/signals";

export class Todos extends Controller {
  readonly filter = signal("all");
  readonly rows = liveRows<Todo>(this, todos, () => ({ order: "addedAt" }));

  constructor() { super("todos"); }

  async add(title: string): Promise<void> {
    await todos.add({ title, addedAt: Date.now() });
  }
}
```

The entry opens the store and then calls `start()` on a fresh controller — `await openTodos(dir,
io)` does both — so the first query finds a store. `start()` runs `connected()` and settles
`$ready`; `dispose()` drops the queries. Views import the controller instance and call it for the
changes they want.

## A view

A view is a `View` subclass whose `render()` returns an `html` template. A signal read in
`render()` re-renders the view on the next frame. A view a template names by tag declares its props
with `prop<T>(name, initial)`, reached by the element's attributes and `.prop=` bindings:

```ts
import { define, html, View } from "@lightstart/light/native/tui";

export class TodoRow extends View {
  todo = this.prop<Todo | null>("todo", null);
  render() {
    const todo = this.todo.value;
    return todo === null ? null : html`<p @click=${() => todos.toggle(todo)}>${String(todo.title)}</p>`;
  }
}

define("todo-row", TodoRow);
```

The app's own view is constructed in the entry and mounted by `runApp`; an editor is a field,
`readonly input = new TInput("textarea")`, placed with `${this.input}`. Keys go through the
renderer: a handler typed `(e: KeyEvent)` reads `e.key` and `e.typed("f")`, and `e.consume()` stops
the bubbling. `runApp`'s `onKey` sees a key before any element and can return `true` to take it.

Tags read like HTML: `div` and the block names are boxes, `p` and the heading names are text, `b`
and `em` are spans, `textarea` is an editor; `key(id, …)` keys a list. The box props are
`direction`, `flex`, `width`, `height`, `padding`, `gap`, `border`, `title`, `scroll`,
`scrollTop`, `stickToBottom`, `align`, and every element takes `fg`, `bg`, `bold`, `dim`, `italic`,
`underline`, `inverse`, `strike` and `hidden`.

## The classes

The terminal is styled by the theme's utility classes (`flex-1`, `border`, `overflow-y-auto`,
`text-muted`, `font-bold`, `line-through`, `focus:font-bold`, …), the same names a page uses. They
are compiled ahead of time into `src/classes.baked.ts` by `light classes:bake`, and the binary
carries the table, not the classes engine: add a class to a template, run `pnpm bake`, and the
test's `--check` keeps it honest. A `class=` value the baker cannot read as literals — write it as
a literal, a ternary or an alternation of literals, never composed at run time.

## Rules that matter here

- TypeScript with erasable syntax only: no enums, namespaces, parameter properties or decorators.
  The sources run under node without a build step and compile with scriptc.
- No `any`. Data parsed from outside is `unknown` and told apart by `typeof`, `instanceof` and
  `Array.isArray`.
- Model and controller code is dynamic data by design; a view is a `View` subclass from
  `@lightstart/light/native/tui` (not the browser entry).
- The store runs in the same process as the views: `openJsonl` opens it and its request carries
  every model call. Open it before constructing the controller.
- Imports between the app's own files name the `.ts` file; imports of light name its subpath.
- Ctrl+C quits: `runApp` takes it unless `onKey` did.

